  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap with its complement when shifted. This is an instance of the minimization of the maximum autocorrelation value, which is a challenging optimization problem due to its non-convexity. Our approach combines gradient-based optimization (to refine structure) with randomized initialization and patch swapping (to explore diverse solutions) and evolutionary strategies (to enhance diversity and prevent local minima). It uses explicit gradient information for local refinement but focuses the search on promising regions identified by randomized exploration. Unlike previous methods, we:\n\n- Use a gradient-aware ranking of shifts to prioritize relevant constraints\n- Combine continuous optimization with binary-like flipping at key points\n- Implement safety checks to maintain the integral constraint\n- Use a multi-stage optimization that balances between global exploration and local refinement\n\nThe approach is designed to escape local minima by: \n  - Using multiple independent initializations\n  - Swapping patches between candidate solutions\n  - Applying intelligent gradient projection\n  - Maintaining a memory of promising regions\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nVariables:\n  - h: n-dimensional vector representing the step function samples\n  - Parameters: \n      n = number of samples (initially 560)\n      score(h) = max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, computed via the discrete convolution\n      0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n      \u03a3h[i] = n/2 exactly\n\nOperators:\n  - Score(complete(h), k): discrete overlap at shift k\n  - TopK(shifts): k's with top overlap scores\n  - BlockGradient(shift, h): gradient w.r.t h that penalizes high overlap\n  - FlipBlock(h, size, position): random flip of contiguous block\n  - Repair(h): enforces integral constraint and bounds [0,1]\n\nConstraints Enforcement:\n  - The repair function uses iterative adjustment to meet \u03a3h[i] = n/2\n  - All flips and perturbations have their integral adjusted\n  - Objective computation always includes max over all 2*n-1 shifts\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nImplementation Steps:\n\nInitialization:\n  - Validate initial_h_values\n\nStates:\n  - Each state is a valid h vector (via repair and adjustment)\n  - Energy evaluation: energy(h) = max_{k}(${overlap}_{h,k}$)\n\nFunction Evaluations:\n  - evaluate_overlap(h): returns max over all 2*n-1 shifts\n  - compute_gradient(h, k): optional but complex to implement exactly\n\nPopulation Management:\n  - Maintain a population of candidate solutions (actors)\n  - Assign energy to each solution\n  - Cooperatively update solutions based on overlap scores\n\nPhase 1: Adaptive Gradient Optimization\n  - For each state:\n      \u2022 Repair to self-consistent state\n      \u2022 Keep top scoring individuals\n      \u2022 Immediately following, apply gradient projection on critical shifts\n      \u2022 Periodically restart from fresh random samples\n\nPhase 2: Patch Management\n  - For each actor:\n      \u2022 Apply small local perturbations (gaussian noise)\n      \u2022 Apply large random flips (blocks)\n      \u2022 Adopt blocks from other promising actors\n\nPhase 3: Collaborative Optimization\n  - Partition shifts by their contribution to the score\n  - Update global optimal state based on mean overlap of population\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nSearch Strategy:\n  - Employ adaptive shifts sampling: focus on 200 most significant shifts\n  - Transition from exploration to exploitation based on cooling schedule\n  - Use probabilistic acceptance of worsening moves (1e-9)\n  - Local aggressive refinement with gradient following\n\nParameters:\n  - Scale activation = [0.0005, 0.002]\n  - Block size = [4, 20] (% of population)\n  - Initial temperature = 1.0\n  - Cooling factor = 0.99995\n\nMemory Management:\n  - Keep track of top 10% scoring solutions at all times\n  - Periodically evict and respawn worst performers\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nResource Estimation:\n  - dominant operations: overlap computations O(n_steps*n) \u2248 1.6e6 * 560 = 896e6 operations\n  - Restarts and repairs O(n_steps) : 15,000 steps\n  - Time budget: 1,100 seconds\n  - Timers embedded in each operation to enforce budget\n  - Adaptive step size schedule\n\nTermination Conditions:\n  - Early termination if plateau reached (30 steps)\n  - Early termination if time exceeds budget\n  - Return best solution found at any point\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nOutput Checks:\n  - Enforce integral constraint post-optimization (repair)\n  - Clip values to [0,1]\n  - Double-check overlap calculation by re-running scoring\n\nFinal Validation:\n  - Ensure all state transitions preserve valid score(h)\n  - Restrict output to a single solution with best composite score\n\nMinimum interoperability specified:\n  - Provide repair function for constraints\n  - Score function that respects all constraints\n\nReturn Contract:\n  - Final solution must be repair-consistent\n  - energy \u2248 score(h) < target value\n  - state validity enforced through gradients and repairs",
